WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:25.300 --> 00:00:30.340
When I write these film analyses, I usually start by watching the film with a notepad,

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.220
writing down all the things I think are interesting.

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:36.700
When I started watching Wong Kar-wai's 'In the Mood for Love',

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.000
the first thing I wrote was:

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:46.620
meaning that every single shot feature characters

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.320
not only framed by the rectangle of the film itself

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:53.700
but by smaller internal shapes as well.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:57.520
Now this is a visual technique that film makers have used for decades

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:03.180
my favourite example is the great last shot in John Ford's masterpiece, "The Searchers."

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:07.000
But seldom is it used so ubiquitously.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.180
As the film went on, I had to cross out 5 from my little note

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:13.120
and change it to 6, 7, 9.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:16.440
It wasn't until minute 12 that I saw the first shot

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:18.220
without some foreground obstruction.

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:21.800
This shot, which crucially doesn't feature either of the two main characters...

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.240
...but wait, I'm getting ahead of myself.

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.660
'In the Mood for Love' is the story of two married couples

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:33.100
who happen to rent rooms in the adjacent homes of older couples in 1960's Hong Kong.

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.220
the film focuses on the husband of one couple, Mr. Chau

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:38.800
and the wife of the other, Mrs. Chan

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:42.560
as they both gradually discover that their spouses are cheating on them

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:44.480
with each other

00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:46.500
and then, how those two innocent victims of infidelity

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.300
come together to deal with the pain of that

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.120
the film manages to be unsentimental with its subject matter

00:01:53.140 --> 00:01:56.740
while couching the events in sumptuous, painterly cinematography.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:01.740
The narrative skips and jumps forward through time at an erratic speed

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:04.120
as one might recall the story in memory

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:09.780
and the viewer is often left entirely confused as to how much time has passed between scenes.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:14.620
This prompts a strong engagement with the film, a need to pay attention

00:02:14.620 --> 00:02:17.200
but it also reflects how the film was made.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:21.480
There was little more than an outline when Wong Kar-Wai and his crew began filming

00:02:21.600 --> 00:02:24.800
a process that took a long 15 months

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.980
in which the script and the individual scenes were written on the fly

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.360
by the director and the actors together,

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.440
which is surprising because watching the film

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:38.600
you can't help but feel that you're in the hands of somebody with complete control.

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.700
Visually and emotionally, 'In the Mood for Love' is fully consistent.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:48.100
The film is so self-contained that it only features a handful of locations

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.600
each filmed from the same angle

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:57.060
so that you experience a kind of circular effect of returning again and again to the same things

00:02:57.400 --> 00:03:03.760
This technique, far from feeling sluggish or repetitive instead isolates against fixed backgrounds,

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:09.420
the things that actually are changing in the film - the inner lives of the two leads.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:13.820
And for the most part, these inner lives are explored wordlessly.

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:18.060
The real action of this movie is in postures, glances and touches

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:23.220
and by restricting the language, Wong Kar-wai echoes the restriction of action

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:26.680
that plagues Mr. Chau and Mrs. Chan in 1960's Hong Kong

00:03:26.680 --> 00:03:30.100
where they are under constant threat of gossip,

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:33.800
a kind of surveillance from their landlords and the community at large.

00:03:40.540 --> 00:03:43.460
This is one reason why everything is doubly framed.

00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:48.020
By placing objects in the foreground the director enhances the feeling that the characters have,

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:53.280
of being observed, not to mention our own feelings of being observers.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.980
Observed and observing, seen and being seen, what we desire from others, what others desire from us -

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:03.080
these are major themes of 'In the Mood for Love'.

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:08.080
I think the most extraordinary element of this film is what's introduced just after

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.320
the two main characters admit to each other: that their spouses are cheating.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:18.640
Instead of confronting the situation head on, their primary goal becomes to understand

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.040
exactly how it happened.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:26.300
So we get this incredible scene where they re-enact the seduction of their spouses

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:31.960
in which each attempt to embody the spouse of the other while being coached by the other

00:04:31.960 --> 00:04:35.880
on what they think the spouse really would, or wouldn't do.

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:42.260
This makes for a really complex, but also kind of perverse interplay.

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:46.680
Though the film aggressively focuses on only Mr. Chau and Mrs. Chan

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:49.900
to the point of never even showing the faces of their spouses,

00:04:49.900 --> 00:04:54.500
we're always aware that four people, not two, are involved here.

00:04:54.700 --> 00:05:00.100
But it's not four real people, it's two people and two phantoms.

00:05:00.120 --> 00:05:04.380
Think of what's being asked of Chau and Chan in the seduction scene.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:11.160
In the guise of understanding what happened, each victim attempts to seduce their own spouse

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:14.160
in the form of the other's spouse.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:19.500
A task that has to be doomed from the start because the fact of the affair itself means that

00:05:19.580 --> 00:05:26.320
neither Chau nor Chan can any longer illicit that kind of desire that they're trying to recreate

00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:29.900
You could describe this course of action as masochistic,

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.940
and Wong Kar-wai lets us feel this by not making it obvious when Mr. Chau or Mrs. Chan

00:05:35.180 --> 00:05:38.380
are being themselves or being each other's spouses.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:43.100
Often we're a minute into the scene and interpreting it one way, before the reality

00:05:43.140 --> 00:05:47.380
or perhaps the unreality of the moment hits us, like a gut punch.

00:05:51.460 --> 00:05:58.320
But because each player is constantly coaching the other, they're able to construct a fantasy in which

00:05:58.320 --> 00:06:00.840
they control their own betrayal.

00:06:02.480 --> 00:06:07.660
And by never consummating their own budding love for each other, Mr. Chau and Mrs. Chan

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:12.820
can infinitely delay the moment in which they have to see things as they really are.

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:15.060
They can stay in their fantasy.

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:19.780
It's a tragic and twisted state of affairs, and Wong Kar-wai has said that if

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:23.680
Tony Leung and Maggie Cheung weren't such beautiful actors

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.660
the darkness at the centre of their characters and their fantasy would be obvious.

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:35.640
Of course, they can't keep it up forever, and in the scene where Mr. Chau finally admits his love for Mrs. Chan

00:06:36.140 --> 00:06:38.840
most of the fantasy comes crumbling down.

00:06:38.840 --> 00:06:45.020
As he says they are like their spouses, which means that the thing they've been refusing to see,

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:52.440
their own lack that they've ceased to be an object of love for their loved one rushes into consciousness,

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.040
like a wave.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:56.440
And yet still, they don't act on it.

00:06:56.440 --> 00:06:59.400
They can only let the brutal truth in so much.

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:06.980
In order to survive, in order to move forward, Mr. Chau and Mrs. Chan doom themselves to missed connections.

00:07:07.180 --> 00:07:12.580
This is why at the end, the film jumps forward to see them just missing each other twice.

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:18.020
Just missing each other, the mere possibility of a connection, not the connection itself

00:07:18.060 --> 00:07:19.920
is what sustains them now.

00:07:20.640 --> 00:07:24.040
Everybody lives within fantasy, within frames.

00:07:24.560 --> 00:07:28.680
Sometimes the frames are made by us, sometimes they're made by others.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.520
Sometimes we need to believe that those made by us are made by others.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:36.400
But whatever the case, there's no way out of the frame.

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:41.580
When fantasies rupture or crack or breakdown completely, that's trauma,

00:07:41.820 --> 00:07:45.400
like living temporarily in a storm.

00:07:45.480 --> 00:07:52.360
Wong Kar-wai's 'In the Mood for Love' is a gorgeous, quiet and painful exploration of what happens when

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:56.340
the fantasy you create for yourself is a perverse one,

00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:02.020
when it only serves to keep you from the pain that it was created to avoid.

00:08:02.440 --> 00:08:09.200
And, the reason the film is so heartbreaking is because this kind of perversity

00:08:09.660 --> 00:08:14.940
is really quite common to all of us.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.540
Hey everybody, thank you so much for watching I hope you enjoyed it,

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.500
God, I love this movie.

